Answer glGetBooleanv from gl4es's tracked state instead of the driver.

gl4es-getter-client-state.patch already routes glGetIntegerv / glGetFloatv /
glGetDoublev through gl4es_commonGet(), because a glGet* that reaches WebGL
is a synchronous getParameter and drains the command queue.  glGetBooleanv
was left out: it had no gl4es-side implementation at all, so every boolean
query went straight to the driver.

gl4es-edge-flag.patch gave it one, but deliberately narrow - GL_EDGE_FLAG
only, everything else passed through - because widening it is a separate
decision.  This is that decision: consult gl4es_commonGet() first, and fall
through to the driver only for pnames gl4es does not track.

Measured with the same benchmark linked against gl4es built with and without
this patch, headless Chrome / SwiftShader, 20000 iterations of
glGetBooleanv(GL_DEPTH_WRITEMASK) + glGetIntegerv(GL_SHADE_MODEL), five runs
per build:

    without   37.2 us per pair   (range 34.8 - 57.1)
    with       0.14 us per pair  (range 0.135 - 0.185)

The ranges do not overlap.  glGetIntegerv was already local in both builds,
so the whole difference is the glGetBooleanv round trip.

The tradeoff is the one the existing getter patch already accepted: a pname
whose tracked value is stale now returns the stale value rather than the
driver's.  Correctness was checked against native GL (Mesa 4.6 compat /
llvmpipe through surfaceless EGL) on GL_DEPTH_WRITEMASK, GL_DEPTH_TEST,
GL_BLEND, GL_CULL_FACE and GL_EDGE_FLAG, each set and cleared - all ten
agree with native.  That is ten pnames, not the whole enum space; this patch
is the one to drop first if a boolean query is ever seen returning stale
state.

diff --git a/src/gl/getter.c b/src/gl/getter.c
index bc123b87..595dc64a 100644
--- a/src/gl/getter.c
+++ b/src/gl/getter.c
@@ -839,13 +839,12 @@ void APIENTRY_GL4ES gl4es_glGetBooleanv(GLenum pname, GLboolean *params) {
         errorShim(GL_INVALID_OPERATION);
         return;
     }
-    if (pname == GL_EDGE_FLAG) {
-        /* CPU-only state (see gl4es_glEdgeFlag): GLES has no edge flag, so
-         * without this the query falls through to the driver and raises
-         * GL_INVALID_ENUM without writing params.  Everything else keeps
-         * going straight through, exactly as before. */
+    /* Only the state gl4es tracks itself has to be intercepted here; anything
+     * GLES knows about keeps going straight to the driver, as before. */
+    GLfloat fparam;
+    if (gl4es_commonGet(pname, &fparam)) {
         noerrorShim();
-        *params = glstate->edgeflag ? GL_TRUE : GL_FALSE;
+        *params = (fparam != 0.0f) ? GL_TRUE : GL_FALSE;
         return;
     }
     LOAD_GLES(glGetBooleanv);
